1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:03,358
This is Vignette. Step 1,
question by question.

2
00:00:03,589 --> 00:00:05,463
Today's set has 2 questions.

3
00:00:05,695 --> 00:00:08,754
Pause whenever you need to,
and commit to an answer before

4
00:00:08,774 --> 00:00:09,779
the explanation.

5
00:00:11,683 --> 00:00:13,110
He fainted on the stairs.

6
00:00:13,341 --> 00:00:15,675
That single detail decides the question.

7
00:00:15,907 --> 00:00:17,531
This one is cardiology.

8
00:00:17,762 --> 00:00:18,965
Question 1 of 2.

9
00:00:19,626 --> 00:00:23,304
A 74-year-old man comes to the
clinic because of a 6-month

10
00:00:23,324 --> 00:00:25,763
history of shortness of
breath with exertion.

11
00:00:25,995 --> 00:00:29,212
Last week he lost consciousness
while climbing the stairs to his

12
00:00:29,232 --> 00:00:31,579
apartment and recovered within a minute.

13
00:00:31,811 --> 00:00:33,158
He has no chest pain.

14
00:00:33,389 --> 00:00:39,013
Blood pressure 128/76
mmHg, pulse 72/min.

15
00:00:39,311 --> 00:00:43,975
Cardiac examination shows a harsh,
crescendo-decrescendo systolic murmur

16
00:00:43,995 --> 00:00:48,317
loudest at the right second intercostal
space, radiating to both carotid

17
00:00:48,337 --> 00:00:51,289
arteries. The second
heart sound is soft.

18
00:00:51,521 --> 00:00:54,750
The carotid upstroke is
delayed and low in amplitude.

19
00:00:54,982 --> 00:00:57,987
The lungs are clear and there
is no peripheral edema.

20
00:00:59,995 --> 00:01:03,934
Which of the following best explains
this patient's loss of consciousness?

21
00:01:04,595 --> 00:01:07,995
Pause here. Commit to an
answer before the explanation.

22
00:01:12,516 --> 00:01:13,969
The answer is A.

23
00:01:14,161 --> 00:01:18,351
Fixed stroke volume limiting the rise
in cardiac output during exertion.

24
00:01:18,996 --> 00:01:22,410
A stenotic valve cannot open
wider when demand rises.

25
00:01:22,641 --> 00:01:26,713
At rest the stiff ventricle still
generates adequate output; on exertion

26
00:01:26,733 --> 00:01:31,529
stroke volume is capped, cardiac
output fails to rise, and cerebral

27
00:01:31,549 --> 00:01:32,673
perfusion falls.

28
00:01:34,630 --> 00:01:38,754
Calcific aortic stenosis can extend
into the conduction system, but a

29
00:01:38,774 --> 00:01:42,345
block is not load-dependent and
does not wait for the stairs.

30
00:01:42,577 --> 00:01:45,689
For B to be the answer, you
would need this: Syncope

31
00:01:45,709 --> 00:01:50,518
occurring at rest, or an ECG showing
wide-complex bradycardia with absent

32
00:01:50,538 --> 00:01:51,398
P waves.

33
00:01:53,318 --> 00:01:58,325
Arrhythmic syncope classically has no
warning and no reproducible exertional

34
00:01:58,345 --> 00:02:02,772
trigger. This patient's syncope is
exertion-linked, which points to a

35
00:02:02,792 --> 00:02:06,654
haemodynamic cause. For C to
be the answer, you would need

36
00:02:06,674 --> 00:02:12,167
this: Syncope without prodrome, family
history of sudden cardiac death, scar

37
00:02:12,187 --> 00:02:15,389
on echocardiography, or
a prolonged QT interval.

38
00:02:17,363 --> 00:02:21,908
Vasovagal syncope has a prodrome of
warmth, nausea and tunnel vision,

39
00:02:22,047 --> 00:02:26,172
is provoked by heat, pain or
prolonged standing, and does not

40
00:02:26,192 --> 00:02:28,908
produce a fixed low-amplitude
carotid upstroke.

41
00:02:29,139 --> 00:02:31,817
For D to be the answer,
you would need this: A

42
00:02:31,837 --> 00:02:36,659
clear prodrome, syncope while standing,
rapid full recovery, and a normal

43
00:02:36,679 --> 00:02:38,197
cardiac examination.

44
00:02:40,063 --> 00:02:43,793
There is no volume loss, no
diuretic use, and no positional

45
00:02:43,813 --> 00:02:48,701
trigger. Orthostatic syncope occurs
within seconds of standing, not after

46
00:02:48,721 --> 00:02:51,898
climbing stairs. For E to be
the answer, you would need

47
00:02:51,918 --> 00:02:57,555
this: A documented postural blood pressure
drop, diuretic use or dehydration,

48
00:02:57,839 --> 00:02:59,252
and syncope on standing.

49
00:03:01,218 --> 00:03:06,527
Exertional syncope is a haemodynamic
problem until proven otherwise: the heart

50
00:03:06,547 --> 00:03:12,409
cannot meet demand. Aortic stenosis,
hypertrophic cardiomyopathy and pulmonary

51
00:03:12,429 --> 00:03:14,539
hypertension are the three causes.

52
00:03:16,505 --> 00:03:18,734
A 95% sensitive test.

53
00:03:19,005 --> 00:03:21,945
An 8.8% chance you
actually have the disease.

54
00:03:22,242 --> 00:03:23,971
This one is biostatistics.

55
00:03:24,268 --> 00:03:25,445
Question 2 of 2.

56
00:03:26,124 --> 00:03:29,183
A new screening test for a
disease has a sensitivity of

57
00:03:29,203 --> 00:03:32,958
95% and a specificity of 90%.

58
00:03:33,190 --> 00:03:36,551
The disease has a prevalence
of 1% in the population being

59
00:03:36,571 --> 00:03:37,182
screened.

60
00:03:39,079 --> 00:03:42,388
If a person from this population
tests positive, what is the

61
00:03:42,408 --> 00:03:45,045
probability that they
actually have the disease?

62
00:03:45,690 --> 00:03:49,090
Pause here. Commit to an
answer before the explanation.

63
00:03:53,679 --> 00:03:54,672
The answer is A.

64
00:03:54,969 --> 00:03:56,264
8.8%.

65
00:03:56,933 --> 00:04:00,900
In 10,000 people, 100 have
the disease and 95 of those

66
00:04:00,920 --> 00:04:07,267
test positive; of the 9,900 without
disease, 990 test positive falsely.

67
00:04:07,499 --> 00:04:16,399
Of 1,085 positives only 95 are
true, giving 95/1,085 = 8.8%.

68
00:04:18,366 --> 00:04:22,781
This is the sensitivity, which answers
the opposite question: among people

69
00:04:22,801 --> 00:04:25,661
who have the disease, what
fraction test positive.

70
00:04:25,893 --> 00:04:28,636
For B to be the answer,
you would need this: A

71
00:04:28,656 --> 00:04:32,608
question asking for the proportion of
diseased patients who test positive,

72
00:04:32,801 --> 00:04:35,437
not the proportion of
positives who are diseased.

73
00:04:37,333 --> 00:04:41,208
This is the specificity: among
people who are well, what fraction

74
00:04:41,228 --> 00:04:45,325
test negative. It ignores prevalence
and runs in the wrong direction.

75
00:04:45,557 --> 00:04:48,221
For C to be the answer,
you would need this: A

76
00:04:48,241 --> 00:04:51,628
question asking what fraction of
healthy people test negative.

77
00:04:53,488 --> 00:04:57,955
A 50% positive predictive value would
require prevalence high enough to

78
00:04:57,975 --> 00:05:00,072
balance the false positive rate.

79
00:05:00,357 --> 00:05:04,954
At 1% prevalence the false
positives swamp the true positives.

80
00:05:05,251 --> 00:05:08,126
For D to be the answer,
you would need this: A

81
00:05:08,146 --> 00:05:11,902
prevalence roughly equal to the
false positive rate, which at these

82
00:05:11,922 --> 00:05:14,520
test characteristics is about 10%.

83
00:05:16,466 --> 00:05:19,998
This is the false positive rate
applied to the whole population,

84
00:05:20,230 --> 00:05:21,498
not a predictive value.

85
00:05:21,730 --> 00:05:23,853
It answers no question that was asked.

86
00:05:24,085 --> 00:05:26,802
For E to be the answer,
you would need this: A

87
00:05:26,822 --> 00:05:30,787
question asking for the probability
that a healthy person tests positive,

88
00:05:30,966 --> 00:05:34,393
which is 10% here, not 0.9%.

89
00:05:36,366 --> 00:05:39,319
Sensitivity and specificity
belong to the test.

90
00:05:39,551 --> 00:05:42,820
Predictive values belong to the
patient in front of you, and

91
00:05:42,840 --> 00:05:44,174
they move with prevalence.

92
00:05:44,406 --> 00:05:48,556
Never answer a predictive-value question
before writing down the prevalence.

93
00:05:50,400 --> 00:05:51,879
New questions every week.

94
00:05:52,110 --> 00:05:54,471
Subscribe so you don't
miss the next set.
